      "text": "To find the positive value of \\( t \\) for which \\( |{-4+ti}| = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), we start by recalling the formula for the magnitude of a complex number. For a complex number \\( a + bi \\), the magnitude is given by \\( \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2} \\).\n\nIn this problem, the complex number is \\( -4 + ti \\). Here, \\( a = -4 \\) and \\( b = t \\). The magnitude of this complex number is:\n\n\\[\n|{-4+ti}| = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that this magnitude is equal to \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\). Therefore, we can set up the following equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{16 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the square root, we square both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side, we get:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 4 \\cdot 13\n\\]\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\n\nSubtract 16 from both sides to isolate \\( t^2 \\):\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nt = \\pm 6\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\), the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
